# Normalise line endings.
#
# Without this, files touched on Windows are stored with CRLF and files touched
# by anything else with LF, so git reports the entire repository as modified
# whenever the two meet - 143 files "changed" when only two of them actually
# were. That buries the real diff and makes review pointless.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` stores everything as LF in the repository while leaving
# your working copy alone. Run this once to apply it to what is already
# committed:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git commit -m "Normalise line endings"
#
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must be LF even on Windows - a CRLF in a shebang line breaks
# the script inside a Linux container.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.sql  text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Binary - never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ico  binary
*.zip  binary

# Fonts shipped with the vendored pdf.js (public/js/vendor/pdfjs/standard_fonts).
# `* text=auto` guesses, and a guess is not good enough here: a .pfb that git
# decides is text gets its CRLF/LF normalised on checkout, every glyph offset
# in the file shifts, and pdf.js renders documents that use a non-embedded
# base-14 font as blank pages. This project has already lost one binary
# fixture to exactly that (tests/fixtures/sample.pdf, 623 bytes instead of
# 592, 31 CRLF pairs inserted).
*.ttf  binary
*.pfb  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
